zed through biopsy of metastasis and location-dependent decisions\",\n      \"explanation\": \"BIOPSY OF METASTASIS is described as sampling tissue from suspected/known metastatic sites (e.g., lymph node, internal organ such as liver, or bone) to confirm cancer after it has spread; it may involve core needle techniques and can be repeated when multiple lesions exist. [Data: Entities (1585, 3479, 133); Relationships (7000)]\\n\\nThe choice of biopsy type is explicitly tied to the LOCATION OF SUSPECTED METASTASES, emphasizing that metastatic-site anatomy drives procedural decision-making. [Data: Entities (4565, 1585); Relationships (9736)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Biomarker expansion is explicitly linked to metastatic disease management goals (finding targetable features)\",\n      \"explanation\": \"EXPANDED BIOMARKER TESTING is described as additional tumor biomarker evaluation recommended in metastatic cancer contexts to identify actionable characteristics beyond initial testing. [Data: Entities (4426); Relationships (9310)]\\n\\nThe stated purpose is to identify TARGETABLE FEATURES—actionable molecular or biological changes that can guide therapy selection—indicating a precision-medicine orientation in advanced disease evaluation. [Data: Entities (4427); Relationships (9310)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Not all related tumors behave the same: benign adrenal tumors are explicitly described as non-metastasizing, while malignant adrenal tumors can metastasize\",\n      \"explanation\": \"BENIGN ADRENAL TUMOR is explicitly described as a non-cancer adrenal tumor type that does not metastasize, and this is directly reinforced by a relationship tying benign adrenal tumors to “not metastasizing.” [Data: Entities (104); Relationships (84)]\\n\\nBy contrast, MALIGNANT ADRENAL TUMOR is described as a rare cancerous adrenal tumor category that can spread (metastasize), and certain genetic conditions are referenced as increasing risk for malignant adr